            {
              "id": "37cdc37c-048b-35e7-9d93-b1aa2ebfb859",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The analysis found that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior when using memory copy functions like memcpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination and source are both pointers without clear capacity or bounds checks. The source and destination are also confirmed to be non-null, so the main risk is the potential for memory overlap, which is not handled by memcpy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a safe memory move function that handles overlapping regions.\n\n```cpp\nif ((cpu_set + cpu_set_size) <= bitmask.data() || (bitmask.data() + cpu_set_size) <= cpu_set) {\n    std::memcpy(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n} else {\n    std::memmove(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n}\n```\nThis code checks if the memory regions do not overlap and uses memcpy only in that case. If overlap is possible, it uses memmove, which is safe for overlapping regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the memory copy function with a memory move function that is designed to handle overlapping memory regions. This ensures safe behavior regardless of whether the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\nThis change guarantees correct behavior even if the source and destination memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e8a628d-b4db-3245-9bb3-f031f781c813",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the length argument is not derived from the destination's capacity. These factors increase the likelihood of a real problem, such as memory corruption or program crashes, if the overlap or null pointer conditions occur at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are disjoint. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately, such as by using a temporary buffer or adjusting the copy logic.\n\n```cpp\n// Example runtime check for overlap\nconst char* src_ptr = reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header);\nchar* dst_ptr = reinterpret_cast<char*>(&cper_data[data_idx]);\nsize_t len = header->record_length;\n\nif ((src_ptr + len <= dst_ptr) || (dst_ptr + len <= src_ptr)) {\n    std::memcpy(dst_ptr, src_ptr, len);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: use a temporary buffer or alternative logic\n    std::vector<char> temp(src_ptr, src_ptr + len);\n    std::memcpy(dst_ptr, temp.data(), len);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, replace the memory copy operation with `std::memmove`, which is safe for overlapping regions.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "51972bad-11a3-33a2-a9c7-7b7177282a69",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The code copies a string into a destination buffer using a function that does not check if the destination pointer is null. The analysis indicates that the destination pointer may be null at this point, and there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but the lack of null checks on the destination is a significant risk. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy, which could lead to non-null-terminated strings if the source is longer than the buffer. These factors make this a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add a check to verify that `cpu_info` and `cpu_info->model_name` are valid pointers before calling the copy function. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && cpu_info->model_name) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination is valid and the string is always null-terminated, preventing undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you want to avoid manual null-termination and pointer checks, consider using a safer string copy function that handles null pointers and guarantees null-termination, or wrap the logic in a helper function that performs these checks. For example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid safe_strncpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t max_len) {\n    if (dst && src && max_len > 0) {\n        strncpy(dst, src, max_len - 1);\n        dst[max_len - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage:\nsafe_strncpy(cpu_info ? cpu_info->model_name : nullptr, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nThis approach centralizes the safety logic and reduces the risk of similar bugs elsewhere.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9f8dc3cc-b596-320a-b123-514c6d1e34b9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `fopen()` is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to use these functions in a way that their risks are mitigated. For `fopen()`, it is important to ensure that the file path is properly validated and controlled, and that the file is properly handled after it is opened.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer way to use `fopen()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_speed.c_str());\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::ifstream` is used instead of `fopen()`. This is a safer alternative because it automatically closes the file when the `ifstream` object is destroyed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bbc5fb24-42ac-3da4-871e-c436dbaf0123",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `fopen()` is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fopen()`, you can use `fopen_s()` which is a safer version. Also, always validate and sanitize inputs to these functions to prevent any malicious exploitation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen()` function with `fopen_s()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nFILE* file;\nerrno_t err = fopen_s(&file, path_max_link_width.c_str(), \"r\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` or `<stdio.h>` for `fopen()`\n- `<string>` for `std::string::c_str()`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fd20e28-e306-3773-9596-5e8a08501aaf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The destination pointer is guarded against being null, but this does not mitigate the overlap or null source risks. These factors indicate a real and actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately or avoid the copy.\n\n```c\nif ((vbios_version != info->version) &&\n    ((vbios_version + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->version) ||\n     (info->version + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= vbios_version))) {\n    strncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case, e.g., use a temporary buffer or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as memmove. Note that memmove does not NUL-terminate strings, so you must ensure proper string termination if needed.\n\n```c\nmemmove(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n// Optionally, ensure NUL-termination:\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6420b844-b1a1-3469-bf3d-8f41319de2d7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source pointer may also be null, which could cause a crash. The destination is guarded as non-null, but the combination of possible overlap and lack of null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. This can be done by comparing the memory addresses and lengths. If overlap is possible, handle the copy safely or avoid the operation.\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->part_number + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->part_number)) {\n    strncpy(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case safely, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping memory regions. After copying, ensure the destination is null-terminated.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
