            {
              "id": "575ef44a-f2da-33c7-b357-78915bf2cf8b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk. There is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the destination is confirmed to be non-null. The explicit null-termination after the call is a good practice but does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer or a safe copy strategy.\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->build_date + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->build_date)) {\n    strncpy(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use a temporary buffer\n    char temp[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\n    strncpy(temp, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    temp[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n    strncpy(info->build_date, temp, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if overlap is possible and you want a simpler solution, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping memory regions. Be sure to handle null-termination manually, as `memmove` does not do this.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
