            {
              "id": "aff4f22c-8635-3534-9d43-33bdc7de302c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source is not null, further increasing the risk. The destination is guarded as non-null, but the source is not. No explicit null-termination is performed after the copy, which can also lead to string handling issues. These factors indicate a real and actionable security or correctness issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct. Also, check that the source pointer is not null before calling the function.\n\n```c\nif (brand != NULL &&\n    ((const char *)brand + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= (const char *)info->vram_vendor ||\n     (const char *)info->vram_vendor + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= (const char *)brand)) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the source and destination do not overlap and that the destination is always null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Also, ensure null-termination after the copy.\n\n```c\nif (brand != NULL) {\n    memmove(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8b953c96-84da-32f3-a412-7b2b8870a2c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the strncpy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which further increases the risk. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no information about its capacity or whether the length argument is derived from the buffer's size, making it more difficult to ensure safe copying. All these factors contribute to a high confidence that this is a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add a runtime check before the copy operation to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```c\nif ((name + len <= proc_id) || (proc_id + len <= name)) {\n    strncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle it explicitly to avoid undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be supported, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping regions:\n\n```c\nmemmove(name, proc_id, len);\n```\nNote that `memmove` does not guarantee NUL-termination for strings, so if you are copying strings, you may need to manually add a NUL terminator after the copy if required.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2848d831-a937-3fec-bcab-5526797940b7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The code uses a memory copy function to copy data from one structure to another, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior or data corruption. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, which would also result in undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check before the memory copy operation to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&header_value + sizeof(metrics_table_header_t) <= (char*)&tmp_header_info ||\n    (char*)&tmp_header_info + sizeof(metrics_table_header_t) <= (char*)&header_value) {\n    std::memcpy(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle it explicitly.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, use a memory move function that safely handles overlapping regions instead of a direct memory copy:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n```\nThis change ensures that the operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f050bab-d8f8-3792-9fa3-e3c6dba22c10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the length argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be safe. The destination is a character pointer, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the actual size of the destination buffer. Additionally, there are no explicit checks or guards to ensure that the length does not exceed the buffer's capacity, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, and there is no risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers at this call site. However, the lack of capacity checks and null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue that could lead to buffer overflows or non-null-terminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the length argument used in the copy operation does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If the size of the destination buffer is known, use it to limit the number of characters copied and always explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Assume 'name' points to a buffer of size NAME_BUF_SIZE\nsize_t max_copy = NAME_BUF_SIZE - 1;\nstrncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), max_copy);\nname[max_copy] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIf the buffer size is not known, refactor the code to pass the buffer size along with the pointer, or use safer string handling functions that require the buffer size as an argument.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation with a length argument of 'len-1' but does not check if 'len' is greater than zero before the call. If 'len' is zero, subtracting one results in a very large value due to unsigned integer underflow, which can cause a buffer overflow or memory corruption. The source is a C-style string, and the destination is a character pointer, but there is no evidence that the destination buffer's size is validated or that a null terminator is explicitly set after the copy. There are no guards in place to prevent underflow, and the length is not derived from the actual capacity of the destination buffer. These factors indicate a real risk of buffer overflow or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the string copy operation, ensure that the length argument is greater than zero to prevent unsigned integer underflow. Add a check to verify that 'len' is at least 1, and set the destination buffer's first character to a null terminator if the check fails. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len - 1);\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    if (len == 0) {\n        // Optionally handle zero-length buffer\n        if (name) name[0] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, use a safer string copy function that automatically null-terminates the destination buffer and prevents overflows, such as snprintf. This approach avoids the pitfalls of strncpy and makes the code more robust.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    snprintf(name, len, \"%s\", vendor_name.c_str());\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "133d62f5-dedc-3991-aabb-fabb956b7c17",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered insecure or unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The `fopen` function is used to open a file, but it does not perform any checks on the validity of the file path. This can lead to path traversal attacks if an attacker can control the `sysfs_path` variable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. In C++, the `fstream` library provides the `ifstream` and `ofstream` classes that can be used to read and write files respectively. These classes provide more safety features compared to the `fopen` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` class from the `fstream` library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(sysfs_path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0c3f9dc9-a2e1-3d7c-92c3-292b68af3001",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will keep reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `schema_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Alternatively, consider using safer string handling functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure schema_key is null-terminated\nschema_key[schema_key.size()] = '\\0';\nfeature_line.substr(strlen(schema_key)).c_str();\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(schema_key, schema_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::string::substr`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4bcffff6-2591-3fec-84ca-559766c225d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(version_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if `version_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent buffer overflows, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Ensure version_key is null-terminated\nversion_key[version_key.size() - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(version_key, version_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and its member functions.\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen` and `std::strnlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7cece13-1c56-3fdf-a0c9-f78ec0f12833",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and neither the source nor the destination pointers are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. The verdict is marked as genuine, and the risk is further supported by the absence of null pointer risks and the lack of bounds or capacity checks.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are disjoint:\n\n```cpp\nif ((p_binary_data + b_size <= cache.data.data()) || (cache.data.data() + b_size <= p_binary_data)) {\n    std::memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle it safely (e.g., by using memmove or by logging an error and aborting the operation).\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, replace the memory copy function with a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as memmove:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n```\nThis function is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely and will not result in undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31bd3edb-d331-3f1d-a44d-ef649b2d147e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in the call to strncpy. The destination buffer is a character pointer, and the source is an identifier, with dataflow analysis indicating that the source and destination may overlap. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard. There is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the function verdict is marked as genuine. The presence of an explicit NUL terminator assignment after the call does not mitigate the overlap risk. The source and destination are both non-null at this point, so null pointer dereference is not a concern here. The risk is specifically due to the potential for the source and destination buffers to refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for strncpy.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((market_name + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= name) || (name + strlen(name) <= market_name)) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error, e.g., log or use a safe copy\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is detected, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by logging or aborting the operation).\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be supported, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping regions:\n\n```cpp\nstd::size_t copy_len = std::min(strlen(name), static_cast<size_t>(AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1));\nstd::memmove(market_name, name, copy_len);\nmarket_name[copy_len] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach safely copies the string even if the source and destination overlap, and ensures null-termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3baba8f8-759a-3970-a5ba-39e8570654b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is not known, and the count argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be safe. The destination is a character pointer, and there is no evidence that its size is at least as large as the maximum string length minus one. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but the lack of explicit buffer size information for the destination means there is a real risk of buffer overflow. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, which further increases the risk. The analysis did not detect any overlap or null pointer dereference issues, but the main concern is the potential for writing beyond the bounds of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum string length used in the copy operation. Before calling the copy function, validate the size of the destination buffer. If the size cannot be guaranteed, allocate the buffer dynamically with sufficient space or use a safer string handling function. Additionally, always explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix: Ensure destination buffer is large enough and explicitly null-terminate\nif (market_name != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "17a0f40a-2e6f-3df8-89a9-ef7793a9a277",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code copies data from a C-style string into a character buffer using a length argument of 'len-1', but there is no check to ensure that 'len' is greater than zero. If 'len' is zero, subtracting one results in a very large value due to unsigned integer underflow, which can cause a buffer overflow or other undefined behavior. The source is a C-style string, and the destination is a character pointer, but there is no evidence that the length is derived from the buffer's actual capacity or that a guard is present to prevent underflow. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy. These factors indicate a real risk of buffer overflow or memory corruption.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying data into the buffer, ensure that the length argument is greater than zero to prevent unsigned integer underflow. Add a check to verify that 'len' is at least 1 before performing the copy. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), len - 1);\n    version[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs when the length is valid and that the destination buffer is always null-terminated, preventing buffer overflows and string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, use a safer string copy function that automatically handles null-termination and prevents buffer overflows, such as 'snprintf'.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    std::snprintf(version, len, \"%s\", empty.c_str());\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures that the destination buffer is not overrun and is always null-terminated, as 'snprintf' writes at most 'len - 1' characters plus the null terminator.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6612adb1-9c85-32d8-a634-43fafeddf460",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. In this case, you can use `std::localtime_s` or `std::gmtime_s` which are thread-safe versions of `std::localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\n// ...\n\ntime_t timer;\nstruct tm timeinfo;\n\n#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)\n    localtime_s(&timeinfo, &timer);\n#else\n    localtime_r(&timer, &timeinfo);\n#endif\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<ctime>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2a44b698-f2c8-347c-8d06-3d625583e114",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a likely vulnerability due to a missing check that the length parameter is greater than zero. The code copies data from a C-style string into a character buffer using a length argument that is decremented by one (sizeOfBuff-1), but there is no check to ensure that the size is positive. If sizeOfBuff is zero, the subtraction results in a very large value due to unsigned integer underflow, potentially causing a buffer overflow. The destination buffer is a pointer, and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the actual buffer capacity. Although the source is a C-style string and there is an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy, these do not mitigate the risk of underflow. There is no risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers at this call site, but the lack of a guard for the length parameter is a critical issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy function, ensure that the length parameter (sizeOfBuff) is greater than zero to prevent unsigned integer underflow. Add a check to return early or handle the error if the buffer size is zero.\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeOfBuff > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer size is zero\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents the dangerous case where subtracting one from zero results in a very large value, which could cause a buffer overflow.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, use a safe wrapper or utility function that performs the necessary checks internally. For example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid safe_strncpy(char* dst, const char* src, size_t dst_size) {\n    if (dst_size == 0) return;\n    std::strncpy(dst, src, dst_size - 1);\n    dst[dst_size - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n\n// Usage:\nsafe_strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff);\n```\n\nThis encapsulates the guard and ensures consistent safe usage throughout the codebase.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a41396cf-ab03-35b4-8b70-4fde0661a347",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors properly. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors properly. In C++, you can use `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(ev_str)` with `std::stoi(ev_str)`. Here is the corrected code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    int ev = std::stoi(ev_str);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument const &e) {\n    // Handle the error, e.g., by logging it and exiting the program\n} catch (std::out_of_range const &e) {\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the `<string>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "91133006-a87f-39e5-a5e1-d357fb130918",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors properly. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors properly. In C++, you can use `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(ev_str)` with `std::stoi(ev_str)`. Here is the corrected code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    int ev = std::stoi(ev_str);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument const &e) {\n    // Handle the error, e.g., by logging it and exiting the program\n} catch (std::out_of_range const &e) {\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the `<string>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
