            {
              "id": "827e3500-579e-3d93-ac37-6a17d28e3ec8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. In C++, the `fstream` library provides the `ifstream` and `ofstream` classes that can be used for file input and output operations respectively. These classes provide better control and error handling than the `fopen` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` or `ofstream` class from the `fstream` library. Here is an example of how to open a file for reading in binary mode using `ifstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(fname, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1a4eaa8-48df-33c3-b7c2-fd9ccca7ea32",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the C function `strlen()`. This function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not perform any bounds checking. If an attacker can control the input to `strlen()`, they can cause it to read beyond the end of the intended buffer, potentially leading to a crash or code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. The length of the original string is determined using `strlen()`, and the new string is written starting at that position. If the original string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` could return a value larger than the actual buffer size, leading to a buffer overflow when `sprintf()` tries to write the new string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that all strings are properly null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf()`, such as `snprintf()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be written to the output string, including the null character. This can prevent buffer overflows if used correctly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```c++\nchar *name = (*kv)[*kvnum].name;\nsize_t len = strlen(name);\nif (len < sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name)) {\n    snprintf(name + len, sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - len, \".smn[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_smn);\n}\n```\n\nThis code first checks if the length of the original string is less than the buffer size before attempting to append the new string. If the original string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will return a value equal to or larger than the buffer size, and the `snprintf()` call will be skipped.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf()` and `snprintf()`\n- `string.h` for `strlen()`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4636ae9-3aeb-37f9-a8f2-67b5480d36a9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `strlen` function in C++. This function does not check for the end of the string, and if the string is not null-terminated, it can lead to a buffer overflow. This can cause the program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sprintf` function is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. If the length of the resulting string exceeds the size of the buffer, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated and that you do not exceed the size of your buffers. You should also consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf`, such as `snprintf`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the buffer, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code snippet using `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \".instance[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_instance);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf` and `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eb2cd348-d530-3f57-93ab-407dc81c48a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the C function `strlen()`. This function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not perform any bounds checking. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to a buffer overflow. This can result in undefined behavior, including program crashes, data corruption, and even code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used to append a formatted string to the end of an existing string. However, if the size of the formatted string exceeds the remaining space in the destination buffer, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities, it is important to always perform bounds checking before writing to a buffer. This can be done by ensuring that the size of the data being written does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\nIn addition, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()` and `sprintf()`, such as `strnlen()` and `snprintf()`, which take an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of bytes to read or write.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t name_len = strnlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name, MAX_NAME_LEN);\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + name_len, MAX_NAME_LEN - name_len, \"[%\" PRId64 \"]\", y);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen()` is used to safely determine the length of the string, and `snprintf()` is used to prevent writing beyond the end of the buffer. `MAX_NAME_LEN` should be defined as the size of the `name` buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf()`\n- `string.h` for `strlen()`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d47146c4-8532-3e70-8447-827a2f71d817",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. In C++, the `fstream` library provides the `ifstream` and `ofstream` classes that can be used for file input and output operations respectively. These classes provide better control and error handling than the `fopen` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` or `ofstream` class from the `fstream` library. Here is an example of how to open a file for reading in binary mode using `ifstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(fname, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ae42019-828f-382e-8ef2-c543c110ffc1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The verdict is marked as 'GENUINE', and the classification explicitly states there is a risk of undefined behavior. The analysis determined that overlap is feasible based on data flow between the source and destination, which can cause unpredictable results with memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null at this call site, which could also lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the destination is not a pointer-based member, making the risk more pronounced. Developers should address both the potential for overlapping memory regions and the possibility of a null source pointer to ensure safe and predictable behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are either completely separate or use a safer function if overlap is possible. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&pmmetrics_version + sizeof(pmmetrics_version) <= (char*)&buf1[12] ||\n    (char*)&buf1[12] + 4 <= (char*)&pmmetrics_version) {\n    memcpy(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before calling memcpy. If overlap is possible, handle it appropriately (e.g., log an error or use a different approach).\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf there is any possibility that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use the `memmove` function instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n```\nThis change ensures that the operation is safe even if the memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "036665bd-f247-356c-9339-825429e7e8c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used as the destination in a strcpy operation immediately after allocation, without any check to ensure that the allocation succeeded (i.e., that the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (strcpy) that will dereference the pointer. The absence of a null check before use makes this a genuine risk for null pointer dereference, especially if malloc fails due to memory exhaustion. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also returned and passed to another call, which increases the impact of a potential failure.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the failure, or clean up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(vbios_version, src);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (vbios_version == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, return error, or clean up\n    return ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;\n}\nstrcpy(vbios_version, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that strcpy is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference. For more information, see:\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "85a292bd-e28e-3713-9212-75aa9edc1ff7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(vbios_version, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA)`, the `strcpy` function is used to copy the `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` string into the `vbios_version` buffer. If the `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` string is larger than the `vbios_version` buffer, it can cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to copy, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strncpy` function, and ensure that the size argument is the size of the destination buffer. For example:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(vbios_version, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(vbios_version));\nvbios_version[sizeof(vbios_version) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ff287b49-e4bd-317d-895e-4f8cc55775f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the size argument correctly represents the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(gpu_vendor_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(gpu_vendor_name));\ngpu_vendor_name[sizeof(gpu_vendor_name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(gpu_vendor_name)` characters from `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` to `gpu_vendor_name`, and then ensure that `gpu_vendor_name` is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa8d34f5-b08f-30bb-886f-bccd30aa8e64",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a strcpy call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (strcpy) that will dereference the pointer. The absence of a null guard before use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate this risk. The pointer is also returned and passed to another call, but this does not affect the immediate risk. Based on these factors, this is a genuine vulnerability with a low probability of being a false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (gpu_vendor_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return an error or set an error code\n    return NULL;\n}\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
