            {
              "id": "e60adcc9-a0be-3b2b-8d85-28095efe06bb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA)`, the `strcpy` function is used to copy the string `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` into the `dev_name` buffer. If `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` is larger than `dev_name`, this can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to copy, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(dev_name) - 1);\ndev_name[sizeof(dev_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(dev_name) - 1` characters from `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` to `dev_name`, and then manually null-terminate `dev_name` to ensure it is a valid string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a0fbe1c-3fe0-3309-b9b3-151c1cb05f8e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer string handling functions that include buffer size as a parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info.container_name, container_id.c_str(), sizeof(info.container_name) - 1);\ninfo.container_name[sizeof(info.container_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `container_id` to `info.container_name`, but it will not write more than `sizeof(info.container_name) - 1` bytes. The last byte is set to the null character to ensure that `info.container_name` is a null-terminated string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb30d248-8c61-3853-ad65-ad3708750aae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or potential execution of malicious code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(info.container_name) > 0`, there is a potential risk if `info.container_name` is not a null-terminated string. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strlen()` function with `strnlen()`. The second parameter to `strnlen()` should be the maximum size of `info.container_name`. \n\n```cpp\nstrnlen(info.container_name, sizeof(info.container_name)) > 0\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the original code. The `strnlen()` function is also part of the C standard library, but it is not available in all environments. If `strnlen()` is not available, you may need to implement a similar function yourself.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "698121a6-d90d-3bac-a372-926809dbf2c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(container_type_name[i])`, the vulnerability arises if `container_type_name[i]` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `container_type_name` is an array of strings, you can ensure null-termination as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure null-termination\ncontainer_type_name[i][sizeof(container_type_name[i]) - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Now it's safe to call strlen\nsize_t len = strlen(container_type_name[i]);\n```\n\nIf you have control over the creation of the strings, ensure they are null-terminated at creation time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6dd7f938-a4ed-3f9d-8c72-ba0e18fc7a00",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a strcpy call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. The absence of a null guard before the first use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The allocation size is not a simple literal or sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero or overflow guards present. The pointer is returned and passed to another call, but these do not mitigate the immediate risk. The presence of a cast does not affect the vulnerability. All these factors indicate a genuine risk of null dereference after failed allocation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the failure, or clean up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (dev_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, log, etc.)\n    return NULL;\n}\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that strcpy is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "06b1e788-5be4-3467-aaae-0a2c320ca462",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null pointer checks before the buffer operation. The code copies a string from a C++ string object to a destination buffer using a function that does not inherently protect against null pointers. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but the destination buffer may be null, as there is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure it is valid. The absence of a null check for the destination pointer is a significant risk, as dereferencing a null pointer will cause a crash or undefined behavior. The probability of this being a false positive is very low due to the lack of safety checks and the explicit risk noted in the classification.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the destination buffer is not null. Add an explicit check for the destination pointer and handle the error appropriately if it is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return an error code\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the destination buffer is valid before use.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination buffer is a member of a struct or class and should always be valid, review the initialization logic to guarantee it is never null. If dynamic allocation is involved, ensure allocation succeeds before this operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name == nullptr) {\n    info.name = new char[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\n    if (info.name == nullptr) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    }\n}\nstrncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n```\n\nThis ensures the buffer is allocated before use.\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\nConsider using safer string copy functions that check for null pointers or provide additional safety guarantees, such as `strlcpy` (if available) or C++ string assignment, but only if it fits the context and requirements. Always validate pointers before use.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "42e975d8-9304-3dd4-b26c-2abd9fceca79",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions like `printf`. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. For example, consider using type-safe C++ I/O operations or safer C library functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with a safer alternative. In C++, you can use `std::cout` along with the stream insertion operator (`<<`). Here's how you can rewrite the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nstd::cout << \"AMDSMI, Success, \" << driver_name << \" found \\\"\" << file_name << \"\\\" and returns:\" << GOAMDSMI_STATUS_SUCCESS << \"\\n\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe original code requires the following library:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `printf`\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `iostream` for `std::cout`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "363a6512-9995-32d0-a44f-b60608b1d96b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using dangerous C functions like `printf` without proper validation and sanitization. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. For example, consider using `snprintf` or `fprintf` with a fixed format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\nconst int buffer_size = 1024;\nchar buffer[buffer_size];\nsnprintf(buffer, buffer_size, \"AMDSMI, Status, %s not found, missing \\\"%s\\\" and returns:%d\\n\", driver_name, file_name, GOAMDSMI_STATUS_FAILURE);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: This library provides functions for performing input and output operations.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            }
